{"units": [{"id": "philos-quote104", "translation": "not to accomplish such a deed for my friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote105", "translation": "not lacking friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote106", "translation": "but ruin stands apart from friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote107", "translation": "and to the friends beneath the earth."}, {"id": "philos-quote108", "translation": "They were revered – but even now they are friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote109", "translation": "I proclaim to my friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote110", "translation": "For they are suppliants to my friends, not to me."}, {"id": "philos-quote111", "translation": "aiding friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote112", "translation": "whenever Ares seizes a friend."}, {"id": "philos-quote113", "translation": "he will gain friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote114", "translation": "friends of the dear maiden."}, {"id": "philos-quote115", "translation": "remaining with friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote116", "translation": "Who would buy friends who are already owned?"}, {"id": "philos-quote117", "translation": "And indeed someone killed a friend."}, {"id": "philos-quote118", "translation": "so that she would not be dear."}, {"id": "philos-quote119", "translation": "to eager friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote120", "translation": "O friend, tell me where there is any strength."}, {"id": "philos-quote121", "translation": "619 O friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote122", "translation": "206 445 598 friends."}, {"id": "philos-quote123", "translation": "854."}, {"id": "philos-quote124", "translation": "O dear women."}, {"id": "philos-quote125", "translation": "dear women."}, {"id": "philos-word1", "translation": "to dear women"}, {"id": "philos-word2", "translation": "overcome by the pleasures dear to him"}, {"id": "philos-word3", "translation": "dearer"}, {"id": "philos-word4", "translation": "dearest"}, {"id": "philos-word5", "translation": "and you, O Leto-born maiden, cover your bow"}, {"id": "philos-word6", "translation": "cover) dear Artemis"}, {"id": "philos-word7", "translation": "dearest"}, {"id": "philos-word8", "translation": "force of Aegisthus"}, {"id": "philos-word9", "translation": "Aegisthus"}, {"id": "philos-word10", "translation": "dear"}, {"id": "philos-word11", "translation": "to friends"}, {"id": "philos-word12", "translation": "much lamented"}, {"id": "philospondos-orth1", "translation": "loving libations"}, {"id": "philospondos-def1", "translation": "loving libations, accustomed to pouring them"}, {"id": "philospondos-quote1", "translation": "of libation-loving drops."}, {"id": "philostonōs-orth1", "translation": "with love of lamentation"}, {"id": "philostonōs-def1", "translation": "loving lamentation, with great wailing"}, {"id": "philostonōs-note1", "translation": "where the scholiast explains it by θρηνητικῶς [in a lamenting manner]. [The adjective φιλοστόνων [of those who love lamentation] should perhaps be restored for φιλαιακτῶν [of those who love wailing] at Suppl. 803, as I said under φιλαιακτός.]"}, {"id": "philostonōs-quote1", "translation": "Pray such things to the gods, but not with love of lamentation,"}, {"id": "philostonōs-word1", "translation": "in a lamenting manner"}, {"id": "philostonōs-word2", "translation": "of those who love lamentation"}, {"id": "philostonōs-word3", "translation": "of those who love wailing"}, {"id": "philostonōs-word4", "translation": "loving wailing"}, {"id": "philótēs-orth1", "translation": "friendship"}, {"id": "philótēs-def1", "translation": "friendship, goodwill"}, {"id": "philótēs-quote1", "translation": "because of the excessive friendship of mortals."}, {"id": "philótēs-quote2", "translation": "he will come into a bond and friendship with me."}, {"id": "philótēs-quote3", "translation": "the things that seem to fawn with watery friendship from a kindly mind."}, {"id": "philótimos-orth1", "translation": "honored"}, {"id": "philótimos-def1", "translation": "venerable"}, {"id": "philótimos-def2", "translation": "reverent"}, {"id": "philótimos-quote1", "translation": "great, honored children of Night."}, {"id": "philótimos-quote2", "translation": "let a reverent prayer fly from my mouth."}, {"id": "philophormigx-orth1", "translation": "loving the lyre"}, {"id": "philophormigx-def1", "translation": "loving the lyre, sung to the lyre"}, {"id": "philophormigx-quote1", "translation": "let a lyre-loving voice be borne from my mouth."}, {"id": "philophrōn-orth1", "translation": "kindly"}, {"id": "philophrōn-def1", "translation": "benevolent, courteous, gentle"}, {"id": "philophrōn-quote1", "translation": "For kindly fawning at first, she leads a mortal into the nets of Ruin."}, {"id": "philokhoros-orth1", "translation": "loving dances"}, {"id": "philokhoros-def1", "translation": "loving dances"}, {"id": "philokhoros-quote1", "translation": "dance-loving Pan."}, {"id": "philtron-orth1", "translation": "charm"}, {"id": "philtron-def1", "translation": "incitement"}, {"id": "philtron-quote1", "translation": "I claim as the charms of this boldness the Pythian prophet Loxias."}, {"id": "philōs-orth1", "translation": "kindly"}, {"id": "philōs-def1", "translation": "kindly"}, {"id": "philōs-quote1", "translation": "he honored a fortunate paean (M has αἰῶνα [lifetime], which Hartung corrected by writing παιώνα [paean]) kindly."}, {"id": "philōs-quote2", "translation": "eagerly rather than kindly."}, {"id": "philōs-quote3", "translation": "this man lying here, kindly to me."}, {"id": "phimos-orth1", "translation": "muzzle"}, {"id": "phimos-def1", "translation": "muzzle"}, {"id": "phimos-note1", "translation": "in the description of the horses of Eteocles."}, {"id": "phimos-quote1", "translation": "and the muzzles whistle in a barbarian manner."}, {"id": "phimos-quote2", "translation": "mares bridled with perforated muzzles."}, {"id": "phineus-orth1", "translation": "Phineus"}, {"id": "phineus-def1", "translation": "Phineus, prophet and king of the Thracians in Salmydessus, about whom see Thes. vol. 8 p. 917."}, {"id": "phineus-def2", "translation": "Φινεύς [Phineus] was the title of a tragedy of Aeschylus, mentioned in the Argument of the Persians, of which one fragment survives, 251."}, {"id": "phineus-quote1", "translation": "the children of Phineus bringing a meal."}, {"id": "phineus-word1", "translation": "Phineus"}, {"id": "phityō-orth1", "translation": "to beget"}, {"id": "phityō-def1", "translation": "I beget"}, {"id": "phityō-note1", "translation": "Thus Scaliger for φυτεύει [he begets], which the scribes substituted almost everywhere for the other form."}, {"id": "phityō-quote1", "translation": "having destroyed the whole race, he desired to beget another new one."}, {"id": "phityō-quote2", "translation": "and Zeus, the protector, begets offspring with his hand."}, {"id": "phityō-word1", "translation": "he begets"}, {"id": "phityma-orth1", "translation": "offspring"}, {"id": "phityma-def1", "translation": "offspring"}, {"id": "phityma-note1", "translation": "Hesychius explains it by τέκνον [child] and γέννημα [offspring]."}, {"id": "phityma-note2", "translation": "Of a son."}, {"id": "phityma-quote1", "translation": "mother-killing offspring, avenger of his father."}, {"id": "phityma-word1", "translation": "child"}, {"id": "phityma-word2", "translation": "offspring"}, {"id": "phitypoimēn-orth1", "translation": "gardener"}, {"id": "phitypoimēn-def1", "translation": "caretaker of plants, gardener"}, {"id": "phitypoimēn-note1", "translation": "where in the copies φιτυποιμένος. τος [gardener]. Lobeck corrected the accent, Paralip. p. 195."}, {"id": "phitypoimēn-quote1", "translation": "in the manner of a man who is a gardener (M has φῖτυ ποιμένος [shepherd of a plant]),"}, {"id": "phitypoimēn-word1", "translation": "gardener"}, {"id": "phlazō-orth1", "translation": "to crush"}]}